BUILD-DISCIPLINE candidate (from the 2026-07-28 scheduler sprint, steps 2-3, shas 1ee8fca/fab5c8d): two rules about SENSOR OUTPUT, not sensor logic. (a) A surface that only appears when something is wrong cannot be told apart from a surface that is broken -- print every time, and always print the count of things checked and found clean, so 'nothing listed' and 'nothing checked' cannot look alike. The scheduler run ledger does this deliberately. (b) A stale record is not evidence of an outage: if the newest record is older than the current arrangement, say the record is stale and let the reader judge, rather than asserting silence that did not happen. scheduler's own row was a 9-day-old FAILED from a job the paced runner replaced -- rendering it as a 9-day outage is how a sensor earns being ignored. Both are the output-side complement to silence-audit's existing input-side rows.
